    This is one of the most common workflows for developers using coding agents — getting a second pair of eyes on your changes before committing.
  + 
  + ## Risk Analysis
  + 
  + | Command | Description |
  + |---------|-------------|
  + | `/risk` | Show the top 15 riskiest files, scored from git-history signals |
  + | `/risk --all` | Show all scored files |
  + | `/risk snapshot` | Save current predictions for later validation |
  + | `/risk validate` | Compare past predictions against actual breakage |
  + | `/risk history` | Show past snapshots |
  + | `/risk predict` | Highlight emerging risks (files accelerating in churn) |
  + | `/risk accuracy` | Show prediction accuracy and per-signal breakdown |
  + | `/risk effectiveness` | Measure whether the risk reflex is actually improving over time |
  + 
  + The `/risk` command ranks source files by predicted regression risk using seven weighted signals from git history: change frequency, recent acceleration, file size, complexity, test density, co-change coupling, and revert involvement.
  + 
  + The snapshot → validate loop measures prediction accuracy: run `/risk snapshot`, keep coding, then `/risk validate` to see which predictions were right (Precision@10) and what surprised you. Validation events accumulate in `.yoyo/risk_validations.jsonl`.
  + 
  + ### `/risk effectiveness` — Is the reflex learning?
